WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.682
Graph neural networks are increasingly deployed in
safety-critical settings,

00:00:04.762 --> 00:00:08.944
but their confidence estimates often become
unreliable when the test data shifts away from training.

00:00:09.024 --> 00:00:13.206
This paper,

00:00:13.286 --> 00:00:17.468
from the University of Michigan and Lawrence
Livermore National Laboratory,

00:00:17.548 --> 00:00:21.730
introduces G-Delta-UQ,

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:25.992
a single-model method for estimating epistemic
uncertainty in graph neural networks.

00:00:26.072 --> 00:00:30.254
By adapting stochastic centering and partial
stochasticity to structured graph data,

00:00:30.334 --> 00:00:34.516
G-Delta-UQ produces better-calibrated confidence
indicators across size, concept,

00:00:34.596 --> 00:00:38.778
and covariate distribution shifts,

00:00:38.858 --> 00:00:43.040
and improves downstream tasks like
out-of-distribution detection and generalization gap prediction.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:49.004
Graph neural networks are being deployed in
high-stakes applications where the test-time

00:00:49.084 --> 00:00:51.579
data rarely matches training conditions.

00:00:51.659 --> 00:00:56.664
In these shifted settings, downstream safety
metrics such as calibration error,

00:00:56.744 --> 00:00:58.595
out-of-distribution rejection,

00:00:58.675 --> 00:01:03.744
and generalization gap prediction all rely on the
model's confidence indicators.

00:01:03.824 --> 00:01:11.018
While the computer vision community has long known
that confidence quality deteriorates under distribution shift,

00:01:11.098 --> 00:01:15.395
this behavior has remained under-explored for graph
neural networks.

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:21.703
This paper asks whether GNN confidence indicators
can be made reliable under realistic structural,

00:01:21.783 --> 00:01:24.020
size, concept, and covariate shifts.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.671
There is a common expectation that adopting more
advanced or expressive architectures

00:01:29.751 --> 00:01:31.937
will inherently improve calibration.

00:01:32.017 --> 00:01:36.280
Through a controlled case study on a structural
distortion benchmark,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:42.197
the authors demonstrate this expectation is false:
graph transformers and positional encodings

00:01:42.277 --> 00:01:47.107
do not meaningfully improve calibration over
vanilla message-passing networks,

00:01:47.187 --> 00:01:50.758
and increasing model size can even make calibration worse.

00:01:50.838 --> 00:01:53.276
This finding motivates a different path.

00:01:53.356 --> 00:01:55.353
Rather than chasing expressivity,

00:01:55.433 --> 00:02:01.900
the paper advocates for epistemic uncertainty
quantification to directly modulate confidence indicators.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.929
The paper makes three contributions.

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:09.658
First,

00:02:09.738 --> 00:02:13.387
a rigorous case study establishing that improving
graph neural network expressivity does

00:02:13.467 --> 00:02:17.116
not mitigate poor calibration under distribution shift.

00:02:17.196 --> 00:02:20.845
Second, G-Delta-UQ,

00:02:20.925 --> 00:02:24.575
a novel single-model uncertainty method that
extends the stochastic centering framework to structured graph data and,

00:02:24.655 --> 00:02:28.304
crucially,

00:02:28.384 --> 00:02:32.033
supports partial stochasticity so that only part of
the network is made stochastic.

00:02:32.113 --> 00:02:35.762
Third, an extensive empirical evaluation spanning
covariate, concept,

00:02:35.842 --> 00:02:39.491
and graph size shifts, across the safety-critical
tasks of calibration,

00:02:39.571 --> 00:02:43.220
generalization gap prediction, and
out-of-distribution detection.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.703
G-Delta-UQ builds on the principle of stochastic
anchoring.

00:02:47.783 --> 00:02:51.885
Each input sample is transformed into a relative
representation by subtracting a randomly

00:02:51.965 --> 00:02:56.068
chosen anchor and concatenating it channel-wise,

00:02:56.148 --> 00:03:00.251
producing an anchored input.

00:03:00.331 --> 00:03:04.433
By varying the anchor across iterations,

00:03:04.513 --> 00:03:08.616
the model emulates sampling from a distribution of
hypotheses,

00:03:08.696 --> 00:03:12.799
and the variance of predictions over multiple
anchors captures epistemic uncertainty.

00:03:12.879 --> 00:03:16.981
Because directly anchoring on graph adjacency would
introduce artificial edges,

00:03:17.061 --> 00:03:21.164
the authors anchor in node-feature or
hidden-representation space.

00:03:21.244 --> 00:03:25.347
They introduce three variants, applying anchoring
at the node features,

00:03:25.427 --> 00:03:29.529
at an intermediate message-passing layer, or after
the readout.

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:33.712
A particularly practical variant freezes a
pretrained backbone and only trains an anchored classifier head,

00:03:33.792 --> 00:03:37.895
delivering inexpensive, partial stochasticity.

00:03:37.975 --> 00:03:42.077
At inference,

00:03:42.157 --> 00:03:46.260
predictions are averaged over K anchors to produce
both a calibrated prediction and an uncertainty estimate.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.245
The evaluation is deliberately broad.

00:03:49.325 --> 00:03:50.769
For structural shift,

00:03:50.849 --> 00:03:58.897
the authors build a Rotated Super-pixel MNIST
benchmark where increasing rotation induces controlled distortion.

00:03:58.977 --> 00:04:04.848
For size shift, they use standard graph
classification datasets including D and D,

00:04:04.928 --> 00:04:09.202
NCI1, NCI109, and PROTEINS with GCN, GIN, and PNA
backbones.

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:11.597
For concept and covariate shifts,

00:04:11.677 --> 00:04:17.620
they use the GOOD benchmark suite covering colored
MNIST, motif, and SST2 datasets.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:24.514
Across all of these they compare against strong
uncertainty baselines including deep ensembles,

00:04:24.594 --> 00:04:29.884
temperature scaling, and Monte Carlo dropout,
measuring calibration error,

00:04:29.964 --> 00:04:35.690
out-of-distribution detection AUROC, and
generalization gap mean absolute error.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:40.075
Across the board, G-Delta-UQ improves the
reliability of confidence indicators.

00:04:40.155 --> 00:04:44.161
On the structural distortion benchmark it achieves
substantial reductions in expected

00:04:44.241 --> 00:04:48.246
calibration error even at severe rotations,

00:04:48.326 --> 00:04:52.332
while keeping accuracy close to the vanilla model.

00:04:52.412 --> 00:04:56.417
Under size shift,

00:04:56.497 --> 00:05:00.502
last-layer anchoring lowers calibration error while
maintaining or improving accuracy,

00:05:00.582 --> 00:05:04.588
with the largest gains on the most severe shift.

00:05:04.668 --> 00:05:08.673
Under concept and covariate shifts it delivers
competitive in-distribution and out-of-distribution

00:05:08.753 --> 00:05:12.758
accuracy alongside better calibration than other
single-model methods.

00:05:12.838 --> 00:05:16.844
And when its confidence estimates feed downstream tasks,

00:05:16.924 --> 00:05:20.929
it is among the best single-model estimators for
generalization gap prediction and is

00:05:21.009 --> 00:05:25.015
highly competitive for out-of-distribution detection,

00:05:25.095 --> 00:05:29.100
with the pretrained variant frequently the
strongest under covariate shift.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.890
The paper carefully ablates where stochastic
anchoring should be applied.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:37.641
On the D and D dataset, which has the most severe
size shift,

00:05:37.721 --> 00:05:44.899
applying anchoring after the readout layer
dramatically improves both accuracy and calibration as network depth grows,

00:05:44.979 --> 00:05:47.974
while earlier-layer anchoring converges less well.

00:05:48.054 --> 00:05:53.694
This leads to a practical recommendation to anchor
at the last layer for size-shift settings.

00:05:53.774 --> 00:05:56.954
The authors also validate that partial stochasticity,

00:05:57.034 --> 00:06:02.058
in the form of the pretrained variant that only
trains an anchored classifier head,

00:06:02.138 --> 00:06:03.965
is both effective and scalable,

00:06:04.045 --> 00:06:09.193
and a neural tangent kernel analysis confirms that
intermediate constant shifts truly

00:06:09.273 --> 00:06:13.990
alter the model's function rather than acting as a
trivial reparameterization.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:18.867
In numbers,

00:06:18.947 --> 00:06:23.444
G-Delta-UQ is a single-model method offering three
anchoring variants plus a pretrained partial-stochasticity option.

00:06:23.524 --> 00:06:28.021
It is evaluated across three distinct types of
distribution shift and three safety-critical downstream tasks,

00:06:28.101 --> 00:06:32.599
and compared against four established uncertainty
baselines including deep ensembles.

00:06:32.679 --> 00:06:37.176
Despite requiring only a single model,

00:06:37.256 --> 00:06:41.753
it consistently matches or outperforms these
baselines on calibration,

00:06:41.833 --> 00:06:46.330
out-of-distribution detection, and generalization
gap prediction.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.457
The core message is simple.

00:06:48.537 --> 00:06:54.887
Do not expect larger or more expressive graph
neural networks to automatically give trustworthy

00:06:54.967 --> 00:06:58.000
confidence estimates under distribution shift.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:02.264
Instead, adapt the principle of stochastic
anchoring to graphs.

00:07:02.344 --> 00:07:07.813
G-Delta-UQ is a flexible, scalable, single-model
framework that produces reliable,

00:07:07.893 --> 00:07:13.430
well-calibrated uncertainty across structural,
size, concept, and covariate shifts,

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:18.980
and its pretrained variant makes it inexpensive to
add to models you already have.
